---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import { audit } from '../data/palette.mjs';
import { REPO, REPO_LABEL } from '../data/site.mjs';
import { principles, comparison, requirements, automatic, PREVIEW_PAIRS } from '../data/rubric.mjs';

// Computed at build time from the same module the pre-build check runs against.
// A hand-typed table of passing numbers would be a claim; this is the check.
const rows = audit();
const passing = rows.filter((r) => r.pass).length;

// The preview and the full table read the same rows, so they cannot disagree.
const preview = PREVIEW_PAIRS.map(([fg, bg]) => rows.find((r) => r.fg === fg && r.bg === bg)).filter(
  Boolean,
);

const proof = [
  ['16', 'Static pages'],
  ['0', 'Client-side scripts on content pages'],
  [`${passing} / ${rows.length}`, 'Colour pairs passing'],
  ['10', 'Old essay URLs preserved'],
];

---

<DocumentLayout
  title="The portfolio is part of the work"
  metaTitle="Colophon | Tyler Young"
  description="The agent wrote the code. I defined what the site had to accomplish, what it could not compromise, and what had to be checked before it shipped."
  eyebrow="Colophon"
  meta={[
    ['Stack', 'Astro · Tailwind · static output'],
    ['Client JavaScript', 'None on content pages'],
    ['Quality gates', 'Evidence · contrast · alt text · links · open items'],
    ['Source', `<a href="${REPO}">${REPO_LABEL}</a>`],
  ]}
>
  <p class="opening">
    The agent wrote the code. I defined what the site had to accomplish, what it was
    not allowed to compromise, and what had to be checked before it could ship.
  </p>
  <ul class="proof">
    {
      proof.map(([figure, label]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="proof-figure">{figure}</span>
          <span class="proof-label">{label}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>The brief came before the build</h2>
  <p>
    I wrote the standard before redesigning the site. It settled arguments while the
    work was still cheap to change.
  </p>
  <p>
    The goals described what a reader needed to understand. The values defined what
    could not be traded away. The requirements turned both into checks. Each
    requirement names who verifies it — a script or a person — because a requirement
    with no owner is only an intention.
  </p>

  <ul class="principles">
    {
      principles.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="principle">{p.principle}</p>
          <p class="principle-detail">{p.detail}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <p>
    I began by reading the previous portfolio as a stranger would: nine essays, three
    project cards, a summary, and three references.
  </p>
  <p>
    The leadership argument was clear. The evidence was not. The site described
    frameworks and influence, but most of the work ended in a claim or a
    presentation. Very little could be opened and inspected.
  </p>

  <div class="compare">
    <div class="compare-col">
      <p class="compare-head">Before</p>
      <ul>{comparison.before.map((line) => <li>{line}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-col now">
      <p class="compare-head">Now</p>
      <ul>{comparison.now.map((line) => <li>{line}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The new order is deliberate. Product work establishes what I can make. The essays
    then explain how I lead the people and systems around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Directing the agent</h2>
  <p>
    The agent wrote the implementation. My work was defining the operating environment
    around it: the objective, the boundaries, the design system, the review criteria,
    and the actions that required human approval.
  </p>
  <p>
    I reviewed every page and every consequential change. I rejected plausible work
    when it weakened the argument, introduced an unsupported fact, or added more
    complexity than value.
  </p>
  <p>
    Working in code made broad decisions cheap to test. A background change became one
    token update. A typography change could remove an unused font from the entire
    build. Every change could be checked against the same system before it shipped.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">Generation made options cheaper. It did not make judgment optional.</p>

  <h2>The quality gates</h2>
  <p>
    Some decisions can be checked by a machine. Others still require a person. The
    build makes that boundary explicit. The first gate is the one that matters
    most here: this site argues that every claim can be opened, so a deployment
    going down should fail the build the same way a broken link does.
  </p>

  <ul class="ledger">
    {
      requirements.map((r) => (
        <li class:list={['gate', { human: r.mode === 'HUMAN REVIEW' }]}>
          <p class="gate-req">{r.req}</p>
          <p class="gate-meta">
            <span class="gate-mark" aria-hidden="true" />
            <span class="gate-mode">{r.mode}</span>
            <span class="gate-how">{r.how}</span>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    Automation protects the rules that can be expressed reliably. Human review remains
    responsible for meaning, hierarchy, and whether the page makes the right argument.
  </p>

  <h2>Contrast is computed, not assumed</h2>
  <p>
    The contrast audit evaluates every colour pairing at the smallest text size where
    it is actually used. It failed twice during the redesign. In both cases, the token
    changed — not an isolated component — so the correction propagated everywhere.
  </p>

  <p class="result-line">{passing} of {rows.length} documented colour pairs pass.</p>

  <table class="ratios">
    <caption class="sr-only">
      Four representative colour pairs, computed at build time from src/data/palette.mjs
    </caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Pair</th>
        <th scope="col">Ratio</th>
        <th scope="col">Needs</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        preview.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class="swatch" style={`--fg:var(--color-${r.fg});--bg:var(--color-${r.bg})`}>
                Aa
              </span>
              {r.fg} on {r.bg}
            </td>
            <td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td>
            <td class="num dim">{r.need.toFixed(2)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  {/* Native disclosure — the evidence stays available without dominating the page,
      and without a line of client JavaScript. */}
  <details class="all-pairs">
    <summary>View all {rows.length} colour-pair results</summary>
    <div class="scroller">
      <table class="ratios">
        <caption class="label">
          Computed at build time from <code>src/data/palette.mjs</code> — {passing} of{' '}
          {rows.length} pairs passing
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Pair</th>
            <th scope="col">Ratio</th>
            <th scope="col">Needs</th>
            <th scope="col">Used for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <span class="swatch" style={`--fg:var(--color-${r.fg});--bg:var(--color-${r.bg})`}>
                    Aa
                  </span>
                  {r.fg} on {r.bg}
                </td>
                <td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="num dim">{r.need.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="use">{r.use}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>What the result proves</h2>
  <p>
    The result is not that an agent can make a portfolio. It is that a clear operating
    system can turn fast generation into work a person is willing to sign.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site is static, accessible, inspectable, and deliberately constrained. Its
    code is public. Its claims connect to running work. Its checks encode the standards
    that should survive the person who introduced them.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the same design problem I work on elsewhere: make intent explicit enough
    that people — and increasingly agents — can act on it without lowering the
    standard.
  </p>

</DocumentLayout>
